question.:question/worked-solution:
Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

First find the partial derivatives, holding other coordinates constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{x}&=2 y^{2} \\ g_{x}&=- 4 x e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}} \\ f_{y}&=4 x y \\ g_{y}&=- 4 y e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=(e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}})(2 y^{2}) \\ &\quad+(2 x y^{2})(- 4 x e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}) \\ &=2 y^{2} e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}} \\ &\quad+(- 8 x^{2} y^{2} e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}) \\ &=2 y^{2} \left(1 - 4 x^{2}\right) e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=(e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}})(4 x y) \\ &\quad+(2 x y^{2})(- 4 y e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}) \\ &=4 x y e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}} \\ &\quad+(- 8 x y^{3} e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}) \\ &=4 x y \left(1 - 2 y^{2}\right) e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}&2 y^{2} \left(1 - 4 x^{2}\right) e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}\mathbf i \\ &{}+4 x y \left(1 - 2 y^{2}\right) e^{- 2 x^{2} - 2 y^{2}}\mathbf j\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the contributions to each coordinate separately.

Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

First find the partial derivatives, holding other coordinates constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{x}&=- 2 y z \\ g_{x}&=\frac{1}{x} \\ f_{y}&=- 2 x z \\ g_{y}&=\frac{1}{y} \\ f_{z}&=- 2 x y \\ g_{z}&=\frac{1}{z}
\end{aligned}
$$

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)})(- 2 y z) \\ &\quad+(- 2 x y z)\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) \\ &=- 2 y z \ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} \\ &\quad+(- 2 y z) \\ &=- 2 y z \left(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} + 1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)})(- 2 x z) \\ &\quad+(- 2 x y z)\left(\frac{1}{y}\right) \\ &=- 2 x z \ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} \\ &\quad+(- 2 x z) \\ &=- 2 x z \left(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} + 1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{z}&=g f_{z}+f g_{z} \\ &=(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)})(- 2 x y) \\ &\quad+(- 2 x y z)\left(\frac{1}{z}\right) \\ &=- 2 x y \ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} \\ &\quad+(- 2 x y) \\ &=- 2 x y \left(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} + 1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}&- 2 y z \left(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} + 1\right)\mathbf i \\ &{}- 2 x z \left(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} + 1\right)\mathbf j \\ &{}- 2 x y \left(\ln{\left(- 4 x y z \right)} + 1\right)\mathbf k\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the contributions to each coordinate separately.

Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

First find the partial derivatives, holding other coordinates constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{x}&=\frac{\ln{\left(y \right)}}{x} \\ g_{x}&=- 4 x y^{2} \\ f_{y}&=\frac{\ln{\left(x \right)}}{y} \\ g_{y}&=- 4 x^{2} y
\end{aligned}
$$

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=(- 2 x^{2} y^{2})\left(\frac{\ln{\left(y \right)}}{x}\right) \\ &\quad+(\ln{\left(x \right)} \ln{\left(y \right)})(- 4 x y^{2}) \\ &=- 2 x y^{2} \ln{\left(y \right)} \\ &\quad+(- 4 x y^{2} \ln{\left(x \right)} \ln{\left(y \right)}) \\ &=- 2 x y^{2} \left(2 \ln{\left(x \right)} + 1\right) \ln{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=(- 2 x^{2} y^{2})\left(\frac{\ln{\left(x \right)}}{y}\right) \\ &\quad+(\ln{\left(x \right)} \ln{\left(y \right)})(- 4 x^{2} y) \\ &=- 2 x^{2} y \ln{\left(x \right)} \\ &\quad+(- 4 x^{2} y \ln{\left(x \right)} \ln{\left(y \right)}) \\ &=- 2 x^{2} y \left(2 \ln{\left(y \right)} + 1\right) \ln{\left(x \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}&- 2 x y^{2} \left(2 \ln{\left(x \right)} + 1\right) \ln{\left(y \right)}\mathbf i \\ &{}- 2 x^{2} y \left(2 \ln{\left(y \right)} + 1\right) \ln{\left(x \right)}\mathbf j\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the contributions to each coordinate separately.

Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=(\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\left(- \frac{2 y}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\right) \\ &\quad+(- \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)})\left(\frac{4 x}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\right) \\ &=- \frac{2 y}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\ &\quad+\left(- \frac{4 x \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)}}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\right) \\ &=- \frac{2 \left(2 x \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)} + y\right)}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=(\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}})\left(\frac{2 x}{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}\right) \\ &\quad+(- \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)})\left(\frac{y}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\right) \\ &=\frac{2 x}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}} \\ &\quad+\left(- \frac{y \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)}}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\right) \\ &=\frac{2 x - y \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)}}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}&- \frac{2 \left(2 x \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)} + y\right)}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\mathbf i \\ &{}+\frac{2 x - y \arctan{\left(\frac{2 x}{y} \right)}}{\sqrt{4 x^{2} + y^{2}}}\mathbf j\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the contributions to each coordinate separately.

question.:question/problem:
Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y,z)g(x,y,z)$, given that

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x,y,z)&=- 2 x^{2} y^{2} z - 2 x y z^{3}, \\ \nabla f&=\begin{aligned}&(- 4 x y^{2} z - 2 y z^{3})\mathbf i \\ &{}+(- 4 x^{2} y z - 2 x z^{3})\mathbf j \\ &{}+(- 2 x^{2} y^{2} - 6 x y z^{2})\mathbf k\end{aligned} \\ g(x,y,z)&=\frac{1}{x y z}, \\ \nabla g&=\begin{aligned}&- \frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\mathbf i \\ &{}- \frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\mathbf j \\ &{}- \frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\mathbf k\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}
$$

Assume $x\ne0$, $y\ne0$, $z\ne0$.

question.:question/worked-solution:
Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=\left(\frac{1}{x y z}\right)(- 4 x y^{2} z - 2 y z^{3}) \\ &\quad+(- 2 x^{2} y^{2} z - 2 x y z^{3})\left(- \frac{1}{x^{2} y z}\right) \\ &=- \frac{2 \left(2 x y + z^{2}\right)}{x} \\ &\quad+\left(\frac{2 \left(x y + z^{2}\right)}{x}\right) \\ &=- 2 y
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=\left(\frac{1}{x y z}\right)(- 4 x^{2} y z - 2 x z^{3}) \\ &\quad+(- 2 x^{2} y^{2} z - 2 x y z^{3})\left(- \frac{1}{x y^{2} z}\right) \\ &=- \frac{2 \left(2 x y + z^{2}\right)}{y} \\ &\quad+\left(\frac{2 \left(x y + z^{2}\right)}{y}\right) \\ &=- 2 x
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{z}&=g f_{z}+f g_{z} \\ &=\left(\frac{1}{x y z}\right)(- 2 x^{2} y^{2} - 6 x y z^{2}) \\ &\quad+(- 2 x^{2} y^{2} z - 2 x y z^{3})\left(- \frac{1}{x y z^{2}}\right) \\ &=- \frac{2 \left(x y + 3 z^{2}\right)}{z} \\ &\quad+\left(\frac{2 \left(x y + z^{2}\right)}{z}\right) \\ &=- 4 z
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}&- 2 y\mathbf i \\ &{}- 2 x\mathbf j \\ &{}- 4 z\mathbf k\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the contributions to each coordinate separately.

